So this is not something that only happens far away, right? This is happening here as well. So I'll just go over this. I won't read every word, but I will kind of go over it broadly. The purpose really is to build public trust, ensuring that

22
00:12:01,437 --> 00:12:24,725
they have plausible identification, and that they can be held accountable. And then it has basically two sections. One is about masking. The part that's about masking says that law enforcement shall not wear any mask or personal disguise except for five

23
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:48,518
things, five exceptions. The first is medical grade masks such as N95 masks. The second is part of gear that's necessary to protect our faces from harm only during the performance of tactical operations in which that gear is typically worn. The third is face coverings worn as part of religious beliefs consistent with how they also wear it when outside the performance of their duties.

24
00:12:48,518 --> 00:13:14,489
The fourth is face coverings worn for protection against extreme cold weather conditions. And the fifth just says that notwithstanding those any mask worn as defined above or otherwise may only be worn with the approval of the chief of police. And the second part is talking about identification that they shall display on their uniform

25
00:13:15,164 --> 00:13:39,481
in such a place as to remain reasonably visible the officer's name or badge number and the name or commonly recognized abbreviation of their employing agency. Then it covers some other items related to these. So when making identification upon arrest, when making an arrest, they shall identify themselves as law enforcement and then state the name of their agency.

26
00:13:40,257 --> 00:13:50,855
Supervisory verification upon request by an individual subject to enforcement action. A law enforcement officer shall be able to provide the name or badge number of a supervisor.

27
00:13:53,200 --> 00:14:15,661
Specialized unit exemption, and this is just that sometimes there are special units, and those special units may be exempt from these kinds of identifications. You know, we might think about undercover operations. Those do, you know, they do happen, and those are special circumstances.

28
00:14:17,095 --> 00:14:39,387
Then we have another section on the duty to verify law enforcement identification. So when a law enforcement officer witnesses or receives a critical report of conduct that appears to constitute a criminal offense within the city of Medford, and such conduct is being carried out by a person purporting to act as law enforcement, the officer shall, where reasonably practicable, request verification of that person's law enforcement identification.

29
00:14:41,328 --> 00:14:55,874
And that might include requesting their badge, confirming their identity through dispatch, requesting the presence of a supervising officer. So these are all things when we spoke to the chief of police that

30
00:14:56,093 --> 00:15:18,605
you know, my understanding, it was a great conversation, it's very helpful, he said, normally if there are any law enforcement agencies, they already have told the police, and so the police department knows, so they can verify. Should there be anything that is not verified, they always would just ask for, it's a normal thing they would normally do. So we're not asking them to change anything, this is just,

31
00:15:18,841 --> 00:15:36,003
normal policy that they already would normally do. So this is conversation between agencies. So this is if the police department in Medford sees another law enforcement officer and says, can I just verify your identity?

32
00:15:36,003 --> 00:15:51,815
So the question was this was a thing for asking what we wanted to understand was what would happen if a resident called up 911 and said somebody's getting snatched up off the street and police showed up.

33
00:15:53,789 --> 00:16:18,477
What would happen? So that was what we wanted to know. We wanted to know could that resident rely upon our police department to identify, to ensure that that is in fact a law enforcement agency and not some hooligan that is pretending and snatching somebody off the street. And so, you know, the explanation was that yes, that it would be absolutely normal, like probably they would already know about it if it was law enforcement.

34
00:16:18,477 --> 00:16:45,528
They probably already know because they were informed. Because sometimes, you know, someone might be being chased by Maldon, and they might drive into Medford, and then they're pursuing them into Medford. And so there's communication between law enforcement agencies. That's quite normal. And, you know, should there ever be an instance where they did not know about a law enforcement agency, then, of course, they would ask for identification. That's just the normal way that it is. So we're not asking them to change anything. We're simply codifying this.

35
00:16:47,283 --> 00:17:16,898
And then there's enforcement penalties, violation of the section where we prosecute in the same manner as misdemeanors, in addition or in lieu to any criminal prosecution, the city shall have the power to sue in civil court, just normal stuff, and severability. If any provision is invalidated, then the rest remains. So that's essentially what it is, our goal. And we did work with a number of residents, including people from Medford People Power and, you know, kind of,

36
00:17:16,898 --> 00:17:35,140
broad groups, folks from ACLU as well, to come up with the original concepts, and then we spoke with the chief of police. So I do feel that this has been vetted quite well, and I hope that we can pass it.

